{- | Native durable blob storage: one sharded CID-to-bytes file per blob, plus
   a sidecar naming that blob's declared MIME type.

   **On-disk layout.** Blobs live under `<data>/blobs`, a SIBLING of the
   repository's `<data>/blocks` (`shell.persist`) and never inside it: a blob
   is not part of the repository's signed block graph, and mixing the two
   would let a blob reach `repoFromBlocks`. Within `blobs/` the sharding is
   `shell.blockfile`'s, unchanged — `<shard>/<hex of the binary CID>`, sharded
   on the first byte of the multihash DIGEST — which needs no adjustment for
   blobs because a shard is chosen from the digest and not from the codec that
   produced it.

       <data>/blobs/<shard>/<cid hex>          the blob's bytes
       <data>/blobs/<shard>/<cid hex>.mime     its declared MIME type
       <data>/blobs/.staging                   in-flight writes, never a shard

   **Why a sidecar.** Content addressing verifies BYTES, not the type declared
   for them (`pds/lib/store.mdk`'s `BlobHalf`), so a blob's MIME type is state
   that has to be written down separately or lost at a restart. It is a file
   per blob rather than one index file so that adding a blob stays a write of
   that blob alone, with no whole-index rewrite to tear.

   **Write order.** Both files stage under `.staging` and are promoted with
   `rename`; the MIME sidecar is promoted FIRST and the bytes LAST, so a reader
   — which keys on the bytes file — sees a blob only once its declared type is
   already there. Each staged file is barriered before its own `rename`, and the
   shard both land in is barriered once after the pair, so neither promotion can
   reach disk ahead of the bytes it publishes. What the barriers do NOT order is
   the two renames against EACH OTHER: one directory barrier makes them durable
   together, so across a power loss the sidecar-first order stays a preference
   and either half can be alone on disk. The reader grades both halves as
   residue for that reason.

   **Directory barriers.** A directory this module creates is barriered into
   the directory `mkdirAllDurably` wrote its dentry into, on `pds/shell/blockfile.mdk`'s
   rule and for its reason — a shard whose own entry a crash loses takes every
   blob beneath it, however durable those blobs' bytes already are. Only a
   creation owes it, so a shard costs one fsync once in the life of the store
   and not one per blob. `blobs/` itself is no exception, though its dentry
   sits in the data directory rather than beneath this module's own: the log
   next door barriers its top directory the same way and for the same reason
   (`pds/shell/eventlog.mdk`). A blob-only write — `com.atproto.repo.uploadBlob`
   is one — advances no repository, so no persist call runs on it at all, and a
   `blobs/` whose barrier came from the repo half would be a directory a power
   loss takes with every blob under it. Leaving it to a caller would make the
   durability of this whole area depend on which OTHER half of a transition
   happened to run beside it.

   **Residue.** The reader therefore skips, rather than fails on, anything
   under `blobs/` it cannot account for: an orphan sidecar naming no bytes
   file, and a bytes file whose sidecar is missing or unreadable. Each of
   those loses at most the one blob it belongs to; refusing the whole read
   would instead make one torn write brick every later startup, with no
   recovery short of deleting the file by hand.

   **An entry nobody wrote.** The layout above has exactly two shapes: a shard
   DIRECTORY named by the two lowercase hex digits `blobShardDir` produces,
   and, inside one, a bytes or sidecar FILE named by the CID hex
   `blobFilePath` produces. Every listing here — `blobFileRead`'s and
   `blobFileCount`'s alike — grades an entry on those two axes in that order,
   which is `pds/shell/blockfile.mdk`'s rule and for its reasons; both
   predicates that decide it are imported from there rather than restated,
   since the layout is one layout and two copies could disagree about which
   directories a store may own.

   An entry whose TYPE contradicts the level it sits at (a plain file where a
   shard belongs, a directory where a blob's bytes or sidecar belongs), and a
   directory beneath `blobs/` whose name is not a shard name, are residue an
   editor, a backup tool or an operator left behind, and are SKIPPED. None of
   the three can be one of ours, since a blob's shard and file name are both
   functions of its own content, so skipping them loses no blob — and the
   alternative is `listDir` on a non-directory or `readFileBytes` on a
   directory, i.e. a refused startup blamed on an unreadable shard.

   What is NOT residue is a plain FILE under a shard whose name does not spell
   a CID, sidecar suffix stripped: this module did not write it, so it is
   refused rather than skipped, naming the path. The asymmetry with the
   directory case is deliberate — a leaf file is exactly what `writeOne`'s
   `rename` promotes, so an unparseable name there is a promotion that
   produced something unreadable, while a directory there is something no
   write path in this module can produce at all.

   **Caps.** Admission is `pds/lib/blob.mdk`'s `admitBlob` and nothing here.
   This module re-checks `checkBlobBytes` only to fail closed on a caller that
   handed it something admission would have refused; that is a bug assertion,
   not a second policy, and it is the reason a blob past the cap cannot reach
   the disk even if a future caller forgets to admit it.

   **Lifetime.** Nothing collects a blob. A blob no record references survives
   every restart until the directory is removed by hand: garbage collection is
   a protocol question (which references count, and when a reference that does
   not exist yet stops being expected), not a filesystem one.

   Nothing here trusts the disk. Bytes read back are returned as raw rows for
   `blockStoreFromBlocks` to verify against their own CIDs; this module never
   claims a row is valid.

   This module is an effectful shell (P14): it holds no protocol state, and its
   signatures carry no `Repo` and no `SecretKey`, so no signing key can reach a
   file through it. -}

import hex.{decode, encode}
import string.{endsWith, stripSuffix, take}
import fs.{isDir, mkdirAllDurably}
import io.{writeFilePrivate}
import lib.cid.{Cid, cidDigest, cidFromBytes, cidToBytes}
import lib.resource_limits.{checkBlobBytes}
-- The shard layout is `shell.blockfile`'s, so the two predicates that decide
-- which entries beneath it a store can own are its too — see `isShardName`.
import shell.blockfile.{fileLeaves, isShardName}

-- Temporary writes are promoted out of this directory, which is therefore the
-- one entry under the blob directory that is not a shard.
stagingName : String
stagingName = ".staging"

mimeSuffix : String
mimeSuffix = ".mime"

-- | The blob area beneath one `--data` directory.
export
blobDir : String -> String
blobDir dir = "\{dir}/blobs"

cidKey : Cid -> String
cidKey cid = encode (cidToBytes cid)

-- | Directory holding one blob's shard.
blobShardDir : String -> Cid -> String
blobShardDir dir cid = "\{blobDir dir}/\{take 2 (encode (cidDigest cid))}"

-- | Absolute path of one blob's bytes.
blobFilePath : String -> Cid -> String
blobFilePath dir cid = "\{blobShardDir dir cid}/\{cidKey cid}"

-- The pure layer already refused an oversize blob, so reaching this is a bug
-- in the caller rather than a rejected upload; it fails rather than writing.
admitted : Array Int -> Result String Unit
admitted bytes = match checkBlobBytes (arrayLength bytes)
  Err message =>
    Err
      "blobfile: refusing to persist a blob pds/lib/blob.mdk would not have admitted: \{message}"
  Ok () => Ok ()

-- One shard directory, created if it is not there, with the dentry that
-- created barriered into `blobs/`, and `blobs/` itself created first and
-- barriered into the data directory when it is missing. See the header's
-- "Directory barriers".
ensureShard : String -> Cid -> <FileRead "_", FileWrite "_"> Result String Unit
ensureShard dir cid = mkdirAllDurably (blobShardDir dir cid)

-- Not `fs.replaceDurably`: the sidecar and the bytes file share ONE shard
-- barrier after both renames (see "Write order" above), which the composite's
-- one-write-one-barrier shape cannot express. The sidecar is fixed-mode
-- (`writeFilePrivate`); the bytes half has no mode-setting counterpart and
-- stays at whatever the process umask gives it (0600 only under
-- `pds.service`'s `UMask=0077`; see `PDS-DEPLOY.md` step 5).
writeOne : String ->
  Cid ->
  String ->
  Array Int ->
  <FileRead "_", FileWrite "_"> Result String Unit
writeOne dir cid mimeType bytes = do
  () <- admitted bytes
  () <- ensureShard dir cid
  let stagedMime = "\{blobDir dir}/\{stagingName}/\{cidKey cid}\{mimeSuffix}"
  () <- writeFilePrivate stagedMime mimeType
  () <- fsync stagedMime
  () <- rename stagedMime "\{blobFilePath dir cid}\{mimeSuffix}"
  let staged = "\{blobDir dir}/\{stagingName}/\{cidKey cid}"
  () <- writeFileBytes staged bytes
  () <- fsync staged
  () <- rename staged (blobFilePath dir cid)
  fsync (blobShardDir dir cid)

-- The staging directory, created if it is not there, with the dentry that
-- created barriered into `blobs/`, and `blobs/` itself created first and
-- barriered into the data directory when it is missing: each dentry is
-- barriered into its own parent.
ensureStaging : String -> <FileRead "_", FileWrite "_"> Result String Unit
ensureStaging dir = mkdirAllDurably "\{blobDir dir}/\{stagingName}"

writeRows : String ->
  List (Cid, String, Array Int) ->
  <FileRead "_", FileWrite "_"> Result String Unit
writeRows _ [] = Ok ()
writeRows dir ((cid, mimeType, bytes) :: rest) =
  match writeOne dir cid mimeType bytes
    Err message => Err message
    Ok () => writeRows dir rest

{- | Write every blob durably beneath `<dir>/blobs`. Writing a blob that is
   already present is idempotent for its bytes — a blob's name is its content —
   and its sidecar is rewritten, which is what lets the declared type of
   already-stored bytes be corrected by a later upload. -}
export
blobFileWrite : String ->
  List (Cid, String, Array Int) ->
  <FileRead "_", FileWrite "_"> Result String Unit
blobFileWrite dir rows = do
  () <- ensureStaging dir
  writeRows dir rows

cidForName : String -> String -> Result String Cid
cidForName path name = match decode name
  Err message => Err "blobfile: \{path}: file name is not hex: \{message}"
  Ok bytes =>
    mapErr
      (message => "blobfile: \{path}: file name is not a CID: \{message}")
      (cidFromBytes bytes)

-- A sidecar is read through the blob it belongs to, so it is skipped here —
-- but its name still has to spell a CID, or the directory holds a file this
-- module did not write and cannot account for.
sidecarOk : String -> String -> Result String Unit
sidecarOk path leaf = match stripSuffix mimeSuffix leaf
  None => Err "blobfile: \{path}: not a MIME sidecar"
  Some base => map (_ => ()) (cidForName path base)

readLeaves : String ->
  List String ->
  List (Cid, String, Array Int) ->
  <FileRead "_"> Result String (List (Cid, String, Array Int))
readLeaves _ [] acc = Ok acc
readLeaves shardPath (leaf :: rest) acc =
  let path = "\{shardPath}/\{leaf}"
  if endsWith mimeSuffix leaf then match sidecarOk path leaf
    Err message => Err message
    Ok () => readLeaves shardPath rest acc
  else match cidForName path leaf
    Err message => Err message
    Ok cid => match readFile "\{path}\{mimeSuffix}"
      -- The bytes are here and the type they were stored under is not, so
      -- there is nothing to serve this blob AS. It is skipped as residue; see
      -- the module's "Residue" note for why that is not a failed read.
      Err _ => readLeaves shardPath rest acc
      Ok mimeType => match readFileBytes path
        Err message => Err "blobfile: \{path}: unreadable blob file: \{message}"
        Ok bytes => readLeaves shardPath rest ((cid, mimeType, bytes) :: acc)

readShards : String ->
  List String ->
  List (Cid, String, Array Int) ->
  <FileRead "_"> Result String (List (Cid, String, Array Int))
readShards _ [] acc = Ok acc
readShards dir (shard :: rest) acc =
  let path = "\{blobDir dir}/\{shard}"
  -- `.staging` is skipped because this module writes it; anything else whose
  -- name is not a shard name, or that is not a directory at all, is skipped
  -- because this module could not have written it. See the header's "An entry
  -- nobody wrote".
  if shard == stagingName || not (isShardName shard) then
    readShards dir rest acc
  else match isDir path
    Ok False => readShards dir rest acc
    _ => match listDir path
      Err message =>
        Err "blobfile: \{path}: unreadable shard directory: \{message}"
      Ok leaves => match readLeaves path (fileLeaves path leaves) acc
        Err message => Err message
        Ok next => readShards dir rest next

{- | Read every persisted blob beneath `<dir>/blobs`, in unspecified order. A
   data directory that has never held a blob reads as no blobs, not as a
   failure: `blobs/` is created by the first write and by nothing else.

   The returned rows are UNVERIFIED bytes off disk paired with the CID their
   file name spells and the type their sidecar declares. `blockStoreFromBlocks`
   is what proves each row's bytes hash to that CID, and a tampered file fails
   there. -}
export
blobFileRead : String ->
  <FileRead "_"> Result String (List (Cid, String, Array Int))
blobFileRead dir =
  if not (fileExists (blobDir dir)) then
    Ok []
  else match listDir (blobDir dir)
    Err message =>
      Err "blobfile: \{blobDir dir}: unreadable blob directory: \{message}"
    Ok shards => readShards dir shards []

-- Bytes files among one shard's FILE leaves that also have a sidecar present
-- in the SAME list, i.e. every leaf but the mime sidecars and the bytes files
-- whose sidecar is missing. A shard's directory listing already names every
-- sidecar it has, so checking membership in `all` answers the same question
-- `readLeaves`'s sidecar-open does, without opening any file: `readLeaves`
-- skips a bytes file whose sidecar is missing or unreadable (see the module's
-- "Residue" note), and this count must agree with that or `blobFileCount`
-- reports blobs the server cannot actually serve.
--
-- Name arithmetic alone cannot tell a bytes file from a DIRECTORY named like
-- one, so this is handed `fileLeaves`' answer rather than the raw listing —
-- the same list `readLeaves` is handed. Without that, a stray `<cid hex>`
-- directory beside a `<cid hex>.mime` file counts as a blob here and is
-- skipped there.
--
-- > countLeaves ["a", "a.mime", "b", "b.mime", "c"] ["a", "a.mime", "b", "b.mime", "c"]
-- 2
countLeaves : List String -> List String -> Int
countLeaves _ [] = 0
countLeaves all (leaf :: rest) =
  if endsWith mimeSuffix leaf then
    countLeaves all rest
  else if elem "\{leaf}\{mimeSuffix}" all then
    1 + countLeaves all rest
  else
    countLeaves all rest

countShards : String -> List String -> <FileRead "_"> Result String Int
countShards _ [] = Ok 0
countShards dir (shard :: rest) =
  let path = "\{blobDir dir}/\{shard}"
  if shard == stagingName || not (isShardName shard) then
    countShards dir rest
  else match isDir path
    Ok False => countShards dir rest
    _ => match listDir path
      Err message =>
        Err "blobfile: \{path}: unreadable shard directory: \{message}"
      Ok leaves =>
        let files = fileLeaves path leaves
        map (countLeaves files files + _) (countShards dir rest)

-- Every file left behind under `<dir>/blobs/.staging` by a process that
-- crashed between staging a file and its `rename`. `.staging` never holds a
-- subdirectory in this module's own write path (`writeOne` stages the
-- sidecar and the bytes as two leaf files, never a directory), but this
-- sweep also tolerates residue it did not write — a stray subdirectory is
-- skipped rather than passed to `removeFile`, which fails on a directory.
removeStagingResidue : String ->
  List String ->
  <FileRead "_", FileWrite "_"> Result String Unit
removeStagingResidue _ [] = Ok ()
removeStagingResidue dir (leaf :: rest) = match isDir "\{dir}/\{leaf}"
  Ok True => removeStagingResidue dir rest
  _ => match removeFile "\{dir}/\{leaf}"
    Err message =>
      Err "blobfile: \{dir}/\{leaf}: undeletable staging residue: \{message}"
    Ok () => removeStagingResidue dir rest

{- | Remove every file staged beneath `<dir>/blobs/.staging` and never
   promoted or cleaned up — the residue a crash between staging a sidecar or
   bytes file and its `rename` leaves behind. Nothing else in this module
   ever removes from `.staging`, so this is the only place stale entries go
   away.

   Meant to be called once, at startup, before the listener binds
   (`pds/serve.mdk`'s `configure`): that is the only window in which a file
   under `.staging` unambiguously means residue from a prior crash rather
   than a promotion this process has in flight. A data directory that has
   never staged a blob reads as nothing to sweep, not as a failure. -}
export
blobFileSweepStaging : String ->
  <FileRead "_", FileWrite "_"> Result String Unit
blobFileSweepStaging dir =
  let staging = "\{blobDir dir}/\{stagingName}"
  if not (fileExists staging) then
    Ok ()
  else match listDir staging
    Err message =>
      Err "blobfile: \{staging}: unreadable staging directory: \{message}"
    Ok leaves => removeStagingResidue staging leaves

{- | How many blobs are persisted beneath `<dir>/blobs`, without reading any
   of their bytes: `pds/shell/server.mdk`'s periodic stats line wants a count
   on an interval, and `blobFileRead`'s full verify-by-read pass is too much
   work to repeat that often. A data directory that has never held a blob
   counts as zero, the same convention `blobFileRead` uses — and, like
   `blobFileRead`, a bytes file whose sidecar is missing is not counted and an
   entry no write path here could have produced is skipped, so this agrees
   with what `getBlob`/`listBlobs` can actually serve. It stats each entry it
   passes over, which is what that agreement costs; it still opens nothing.

   Agreement is about WHICH BLOBS and not about refusing: this never parses a
   leaf name as a CID, so a file `blobFileRead` refuses over is simply not
   counted here. That costs nothing, because a server reads the blobs before
   it serves any and this count is a stats line on an interval — the refusal
   has already happened somewhere a caller acts on. -}
export
blobFileCount : String -> <FileRead "_"> Result String Int
blobFileCount dir =
  if not (fileExists (blobDir dir)) then
    Ok 0
  else match listDir (blobDir dir)
    Err message =>
      Err "blobfile: \{blobDir dir}: unreadable blob directory: \{message}"
    Ok shards => countShards dir shards
